# Portal timezone (Pacific Time)

Arbor Live Portal is **always** Pacific Time. Do not use the browser/OS timezone for display, parsing, calendar grids, or persisted wall-clock inputs.

## Canonical source

- Constant: `PORTAL_TIMEZONE` (`"America/Los_Angeles"`) in `packages/format` (`@arbor/format`)
- Web re-export: `@/lib/format` and helpers in `@/lib/crew-availability`

## Do

- **Display:** `formatDate`, `formatDateTime`, `formatDateTimeRange` from `@/lib/format` / `@arbor/format`
- **Hydrate inputs from ms:** `toPacificDateTimeInput` / `toLocalDateTimeInput` (crew-availability wrapper)
- **Persist input strings → ms:** `pacificDateTimeInputToMs` / `localDateTimeInputToMs` / `requireLocalDateTimeInputMs`
- **Date + time strings:** `pacificDateAndTimeToMs`, `pacificDateKey`
- **FullCalendar:** set `timeZone={PORTAL_TIMEZONE}` (see `events-calendar-view.tsx`)
- **Date-only calendars:** treat `YYYY-MM-DD` as Pacific calendar dates; parse/format via `@arbor/format` when converting to/from ms

## Do not

- `toLocaleString()` / `toLocaleDateString()` / `toLocaleTimeString()` without `timeZone: PORTAL_TIMEZONE`
- `new Date("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm").getTime()` or `new Date(input).getTime()` to save wall-clock values (that uses **browser** local)
- `getHours()` / `getFullYear()` etc. on stored instants to build input strings (use `toPacificDateTimeInput`)
- Assume the user's machine is in Pacific

## Picker nuance

`DateTimePicker` keeps **naive wall-clock digits** in the UI string (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm`). Those digits mean Pacific; convert only at the ms boundary with the helpers above.

## Web UI copy

Do **not** label times as “Pacific”, “PT”, “PST”, “PDT”, or “America/Los_Angeles” in user-facing web UI. The whole app is Pacific by convention — redundant timezone chrome confuses users and keeps getting re-added. Keep timezone names in code, docs for engineers, and emails only when an external recipient might need the zone.
